<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarajevo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarajevo is the principal city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, which came under Austro-Hungarian administration in 1878 and was annexed in 1908. On 28 June 1914 the heir to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> throne, Archduke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria">Franz Ferdinand</a>, was assassinated there by the Bosnian Serb nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavrilo-princip">Gavrilo Princip</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> identify the assassination as the trigger of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-crisis">July Crisis</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, which destroyed the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarajevo.jpg" alt="Sarajevo" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julian Nyča · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarajevo_City_Panorama.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sarajevo lies near the geometric centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, in the Sarajevo valley in the middle of the Dinaric Alps, along the Miljacka River, 518 metres above sea level. Forested hills and five major mountains surround it: Treskavica, Bjelašnica, Jahorina, Trebević and Igman. The last four are known as the Olympic Mountains of Sarajevo, because venues for the 1984 Winter Olympics were built on them. The name comes from the Turkish saray, meaning palace or mansion, and scholars disagree on the origin of the ending evo. The official name under Ottoman rule was Saraybosna, the Palace of Bosnia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>One of the earliest settlements in the area is that of the Neolithic Butmir culture, discovered in 1893 at Ilidža by Austro-Hungarian authorities building an agricultural school. The area's flint attracted settlers, and the Butmir people developed distinctive ceramics. The modern city arose in the fifteenth century as an Ottoman stronghold, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> extended into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Isa-Beg Ishaković called the town he was going to construct Šeher, Turkish for city. Gazi Husrev-beg established a school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> philosophy in 1537, the first institution that can be classed as tertiary education.</p><p>Ottoman rule lasted until 1878 de facto and 1908 de jure, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> then governed to 1918. Muslims, Orthodox Christians, Roman Catholics and Sephardi Jews shared the city under Ottoman rule while keeping distinct identities, and by 1909 about 50 per cent of its people were Muslim, 25 per cent Catholic, 15 per cent Orthodox and 10 per cent Jewish. In 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavrilo-princip">Gavrilo Princip</a>, a local Young Bosnia activist, assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo, a murder that sparked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Austro-Hungarian rule ended and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> was created.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the city became the capital of the Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and grew rapidly. It hosted the 1984 Winter Olympics. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a> it endured the longest siege of a capital city in modern history, 1,425 days from April 1992 to February 1996. The 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 527,049 people in the city's ten municipalities, 49.2 per cent of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a>, 29.8 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and 6.6 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>. The war displaced hundreds of thousands, most of whom have not returned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sarajevo is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina, and the de jure capital of Republika Srpska. All national institutions are there. It is one of the few major European cities with a mosque, a Catholic church, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church and a synagogue in the same neighbourhood, and has been called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Gazi Husrev-beg Mosque and the Sarajevo cathedral are among its landmarks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarajevo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The assassination of the heir to the throne, Archduke Franz Ferdinand, in <b>Sarajevo</b> on 28 June 1914 by the Bosnian Serb nationalist Gavrilo Princip triggered the July Crisis that produced the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Germans took the Ljubija iron mines, the <b>Sarajevo</b>–Tuzla–Zenica industrial complex and the bauxite, and about 200,000 citizens of the NDH were sent to Germany as slave and forced labourers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On 28 June 1914 Archduke Franz Ferdinand was assassinated in <b>Sarajevo</b>, an act behind which stood the secret Serbian officers' organisation Black Hand, which aimed to break the South Slav provinces away from Austria-Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cominform chose 28 June 1948 to expel Yugoslavia: the anniversary of the Battle of Kosovo Field in 1389, the assassination of Archduke Ferdinand at <b>Sarajevo</b> in 1914 and the Vidovdan Constitution of 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…in Incheon in November 2022, at a powerlifting championship in Dubai and at an ice hockey match in <b>Sarajevo</b> in February 2023; Chief Executive John Lee called it closely connected with the 2019 violence and with advocacy of independence, and Google declined the government's request to change its…</blockquote><a href="/songs/glory-to-hong-kong">Glory to Hong Kong</a></li><li><blockquote>Germany signed the peace in the Hall of Mirrors, five years to the day after <b>Sarajevo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
